     "up": {"title":"Inside an expert system (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A livestock service in Guyana is building an expert system to help farmers diagnose cattle diseases. Match each part, stage or feature to what it does. Seven pairs, including two new ideas.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Knowledge acquisition","r":"The stage when a knowledge engineer interviews veterinarians and records how they reach a diagnosis"},{"l":"Knowledge base","r":"Stores facts and rules such as \"IF milk yield drops AND the udder is swollen THEN suspect mastitis\""},{"l":"Inference engine","r":"Chains the rules together, using each answer the farmer gives to decide which question to ask next"},{"l":"Explanation facility","r":"Shows the farmer: \"I concluded mastitis because you said the udder is swollen and the milk is clotted\""},{"l":"User interface","r":"Presents short yes/no questions in plain language on a tablet in the field"},{"l":"Domain expert","r":"The veterinary surgeon whose years of diagnostic experience the system copies"},{"l":"A key limitation","r":"It cannot recognise a new disease that none of its rules describe"}],"win":"Very good. You can now describe how knowledge gets into an expert system, how it reasons and explains, and where its limits lie."}
