     "up": {"title":"Management by exception (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The manager of the Harbour Fuel depot in Paramaribo receives a weekly summarised report of the average fuel level across all 40 tanks. One tank leaked slowly for three weeks before anyone noticed, because the average hardly moved. Which change to the reporting would BEST have caught the leak early?","options":[{"t":"A detailed report of every hourly reading for every tank, emailed each day","fb":"All the evidence would be there, but buried in about 6,700 lines a week. She has no time to read it, so the leak could still be missed."},{"t":"An ad hoc report that she runs whenever she remembers to check","fb":"Ad hoc reports answer one-off questions. A leak needs a routine check that does not depend on her memory."},{"t":"The same summarised report, but sent daily instead of weekly","fb":"Sending it more often does not help. An average across 40 tanks still hides one tank losing fuel slowly."},{"t":"A daily exception report listing only tanks whose level fell by more than 2 per cent overnight when no fuel was issued","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A scheduled exception report with a sensible limit puts the one abnormal tank in front of her the next morning: management by exception."}]}
