     "up": {"title":"When one component fails (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A hotel group in Grenada moved its booking database to a cloud server. The software works, the PCs are new, staff are trained and the procedures are clear. Yet the Grand Anse and Carriacou hotels sometimes see different room availability for up to an hour, and guests are double-booked. Which component is MOST likely at fault?","options":[{"t":"Users: staff are entering bookings incorrectly","fb":"Staff are trained and follow the procedures. Correct entries can still clash if the sites cannot see each other's bookings in time."},{"t":"Data: the room records on the server are corrupt","fb":"The records are correct on the server; they are late reaching the branches. The data is a symptom, not the cause."},{"t":"Procedures: staff need a written cancellation routine","fb":"The procedures are already clear, and both hotels follow them. The problem is that their screens disagree."},{"t":"Network: the link that keeps each hotel in step with the cloud server is slow or drops out, so each hotel works from out-of-date data","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. When the connection to the shared server is unreliable, each site sees an old copy of availability, and double bookings follow."}]}
