     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> An information system is an organised combination of hardware, software, data, procedures, users and a network that work together to collect, process, store and distribute information in order to support the operations, management and decision making of an organisation.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> A <b>transaction processing system</b> records the group's routine, day-to-day transactions. It is used by front-desk clerks and reservation agents. Its major input is transaction data such as a guest's check-in details and card payment, and its major output is processed transactions: a receipt, an updated room status and an updated guest account.</p>\n<p>A <b>management information system</b> uses the summarised transaction data from the TPS to produce scheduled reports for middle managers, here the manager of each hotel. Its input is the week's check-ins and payments totalled from the TPS, and its output is information such as a weekly occupancy summary and an exception report of under-booked nights.</p>\n<p>A <b>decision support system</b> is an interactive system that uses models and what-if analysis to support semi-structured decisions. It is used by the revenue manager. Its input is occupancy history from the TPS and MIS plus external data such as expected airline and cruise arrivals, and its output is a forecast of occupancy and revenue at different room rates, which helps the manager set next season's prices.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A summarised report condenses all the records into totals or averages, for example the total and average occupancy for each of the three hotels this week, whereas an exception report shows only the records that fall outside a set limit, for example only the nights on which a hotel was below 50 per cent occupancy. The summary gives the full picture; the exception report draws the manager's attention straight to the problems.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> An expert system captures the knowledge of experienced air-conditioning engineers as IF ... THEN rules in a knowledge base. A junior technician (a non-expert) enters the symptoms, such as \"unit runs but does not cool\" and \"ice on the coil\", and answers the system's questions. The inference engine applies the rules and outputs a diagnosis, such as low refrigerant or a blocked filter, with a recommended repair and an explanation of its reasoning.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>Part (b) is where most marks are lost: students describe each system but forget the personnel or the input and output the question asks for. Treat the question's list as a checklist and tick off each item for each system. Always use the stimulus details: a generic answer about \"a business\" caps your marks.</p>",
