      "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Hardware: the till and label printer. Software: the pharmacy management program. Data: patient names and drug codes. Procedures: the written steps for the nightly backup. Users: the pharmacist. Network: the router and Internet connection.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> (i) Software is a set of instructions for the machine (the hardware), whereas procedures are instructions for the people. (ii) The pharmacist follows the procedures to use the hardware and software correctly.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> (i) The component that failed was the network. (ii) Because of this, the pharmacy could not get card payments approved by the card-payment provider or order new stock from its suppliers, even though its computers worked.</p>",
      "teacherTip": "<p>The word bank removes the recall load so the student can focus on the six component names and on the key relationship: software instructs machines, procedures instruct people. Once secure, ask them to rewrite part (b) as full linked sentences for the 15-mark version.</p>"
