     "up": {"title":"Peripheral or core? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These cases test the definition at its edges: built-in devices, wireless links and drivers. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"On a laptop, the webcam is built into the lid. Is it part of the core?","options":["No: it is outside the CPU and main memory, so it does the job of an input peripheral even though it is built in","Yes, because it is inside the case","It is a storage peripheral"],"answer":0,"fb":"The core is the CPU and main memory. Being built in does not change the webcam's role."},{"text":"Which of these is part of the core, NOT a peripheral?","options":["A Bluetooth headset","A USB fingerprint reader","The cache memory on the processor chip"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cache sits with the CPU at the core of the computer."},{"text":"A Bluetooth barcode scanner connects with no cable at all. Is it still a peripheral?","options":["No, peripherals must use cables","Only while it is charging","Yes: it is connected to and controlled by the computer, outside the core; the type of link does not matter"],"answer":2,"fb":"Wired or wireless, the scanner is outside the core and controlled by the computer."},{"text":"A new scanner scans straight away, but its \"scan to searchable PDF\" button does nothing until the maker's software is installed. Why?","options":["The scanner is faulty","Plug and play loaded a basic driver; the full driver and software add the extra features","The CPU is too slow"],"answer":1,"fb":"Generic drivers give basic functions; the manufacturer's driver unlocks the rest."},{"text":"A 4G USB modem lets a field officer in rural Guyana send her reports. Classify it.","options":["Input peripheral","Communication peripheral","Storage peripheral"],"answer":1,"fb":"It adds a way to communicate: a communication peripheral."},{"text":"Why can a printer be called BOTH an output device and a peripheral?","options":["Because it has its own memory","\"Output\" describes its function; \"peripheral\" describes its position outside the core","Because printers are always wireless"],"answer":1,"fb":"The two words answer different questions: what it does, and where it sits in the system."}],"win":"Excellent. Position (core or peripheral) and function (input, output, storage, communication) are separate labels, and drivers decide what a peripheral can actually do."}
