     "up": {"title":"Where does the app come from? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Now judge which distribution or source fits each organisation's needs, and spot the risks.</p>","items":[{"text":"A school in Roseau wants an office suite for 40 lab computers with no licence cost, and is happy to use community forums for help. Which source fits BEST?","options":["Open-source","Bespoke","Proprietary, published separately"],"answer":0,"fb":"No licence fee and community support suit a school on a tight budget."},{"text":"An accounting firm in Bridgetown must file tax returns on deadline and wants guaranteed updates whenever tax rules change. Which source fits BEST?","options":["A university project","A proprietary package with vendor support","An open-source tool whose only volunteer has stopped working on it"],"answer":1,"fb":"Guaranteed updates and a support line are a proprietary strength that matters at deadlines."},{"text":"A bus-timetable app built as a university project becomes popular, but its student developers graduate and move away. What is the MAIN risk?","options":["It turns into an embedded system","It stops receiving fixes and updates","It becomes proprietary"],"answer":1,"fb":"When the people who wrote it leave, nobody may maintain it."},{"text":"A new phone comes with a maps app already installed. That app was...","options":["published separately","bundled","bespoke"],"answer":1,"fb":"Supplied with the device or its operating system: bundled."},{"text":"A ferry company in St. Vincent buys a ticketing package from a vendor and installs it on its own computers. Its distribution is...","options":["embedded","bundled","published separately"],"answer":2,"fb":"Bought and installed on its own: published separately."},{"text":"Which statement about open-source software is MOST accurate?","options":["Its source code is published, although some firms charge for support","Businesses are not allowed to use it","It is always free, and nobody ever pays for anything connected to it"],"answer":0,"fb":"Red Hat is the classic example: open-source code, paid support subscriptions."}],"win":"Well judged. The best source depends on the organisation: budget and flexibility favour open-source, guaranteed support favours proprietary, and university projects carry a maintenance risk."}
