     "up": {"title":"Spot the operating system at work (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A clerk at a shipping agency in Ladyville describes a busy morning on the office network. Some events are the work of the operating system; others belong to applications, utilities or language translators. Read carefully: several lines are close calls.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that describes a function of the OPERATING SYSTEM itself.","lines":["1. Three staff send documents to the one office printer at once; the jobs are queued on the disk and printed in turn.","2. The accounts spreadsheet recalculates the VAT column when a price is changed.","3. A newly delivered barcode scanner works once the system loads its device driver.","4. A separately installed antivirus utility scans an emailed invoice and quarantines a macro.","5. A junior clerk can open the shared Payroll folder but cannot delete anything in it, because of the access rights on her account.","6. A Python script written by the IT officer is translated and run one statement at a time.","7. RAM is full, so the data of an idle program is moved to a page file on the SSD to make room for a new one.","8. The laptop joins the office Wi-Fi on its own and obtains an address so that the browser can reach the Internet.","9. A ZIP program compresses a folder of scanned bills of lading for emailing.","10. When the email program stops responding, a message reports the problem, offers to close it and records the event in a log."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7,9],"win":"Precise work. Spooling (device management), drivers, access rights (security), virtual memory (memory management), network connection management and error handling are all OS functions. The spreadsheet is application software, antivirus and ZIP tools are utilities, and the Python interpreter is a language translator.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask: is this the OS managing the computer's resources all the time, or a separate program (application, utility or translator) doing one specific job? Antivirus and compression are utilities even when they come with the computer."}
