     "up": {"title":"Match the operating system type (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Each situation describes how a computer is being used, not the name of its operating system. Decide which type of OS the situation needs. Watch for the differences between multitasking, multi-user and multi-programming.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Batch","r":"Salary data for 3,000 public servants in Basseterre is collected during the day and processed in one run at midnight with no operator input"},{"l":"Real-time","r":"The braking controller in a minibus in Kingston must respond to wheel-sensor readings within milliseconds, or its output is useless"},{"l":"Multi-user","r":"Thirty tellers at a bank in Port of Spain log in at the same time to one central computer, each in their own session with their own access rights"},{"l":"Multi-programming","r":"While one job waits for the disk, the CPU switches to another job already held in main memory, so the processor is never left idle"},{"l":"Single-user, multitasking","r":"A graphic designer in Oranjestad edits a poster, streams music and renders a video on her own desktop at the same time"},{"l":"Mobile","r":"A fisher in Grenville checks weather alerts on a touchscreen device built to save battery and run apps downloaded from a store"}],"win":"Well reasoned. The type is decided by how many users share the machine at once, whether anyone interacts during processing, how quickly it must respond, and how it keeps the CPU busy. Multitasking is one user with many programs; multi-user is many users on one machine; multi-programming is about keeping the processor busy while jobs wait."}
