     "up": {"title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are reviewing the embedded devices of businesses across the region. Each answer needs reasoning about connection, updates and risk. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A hotel in Rodney Bay connects its smart door locks to the same network as guest Wi-Fi. What is the most direct threat?","options":["The locks will use up the guests' bandwidth","The locks will stop working when the Wi-Fi password changes","A guest's infected laptop could reach the locks and attack them","Guests will be able to see the hotel's booking system on their phones"],"answer":2,"fb":"Sharing a network means any device on it, including an infected guest laptop, can reach the locks."},{"text":"In the Target breach, attackers first got in using credentials stolen from a heating and air-conditioning contractor. What does this BEST show?","options":["Card data is only at risk when customers pay online","Contractors should never be allowed to use computers","Air-conditioning units are general-purpose computers","A connection that seems unrelated can lead to the tills, so payment systems need a separate network"],"answer":3,"fb":"The attackers moved from the contractor's access to the tills; segmentation would have blocked that path."},{"text":"Why can a shop owner in Basseterre not simply install antivirus on her card terminal?","options":["Antivirus would break the card's chip","Its firmware is fixed and it does not accept extra programs; fixes must come from the maker","Card terminals have no processor","Antivirus only works on Apple computers"],"answer":1,"fb":"Embedded devices run fixed firmware for one job, so protection depends on the maker's updates and on how the device is connected."},{"text":"A CCTV camera in a Kingston warehouse still uses the factory password \"admin\". What is the BEST evaluation?","options":["High risk, but only if the camera records sound","Low risk, because cameras hold no customer data","High risk: default passwords are widely published, so anyone who reaches the camera can log in","Low risk, as long as the camera is switched on"],"answer":2,"fb":"Default credentials are the easiest way in; the Mirai malware spread through exactly such cameras."},{"text":"Which of these is NOT an embedded system?","options":["A card terminal at a Grenada supermarket","The controller in a microwave","A smart electricity meter in a Port of Spain home","A waiter's tablet that runs a menu app, email and a web browser"],"answer":3,"fb":"A tablet running many kinds of program is a general-purpose computer."},{"text":"The maker of the tills at a supermarket chain in Barbados announces it will stop issuing firmware updates. What should the chain do?","options":["Nothing, since the tills still take payments correctly","Turn off the receipt printers to reduce risk","Connect the tills directly to the Internet so they can download updates from other makers","Isolate the tills on their own network now and plan to replace them, because new flaws will never be fixed"],"answer":3,"fb":"Unsupported connected devices are lingering vulnerabilities; isolate them in the short term and replace them in the long term."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. Connection makes embedded devices reachable, fixed firmware makes them hard to patch, and default passwords make them easy to enter. Separate networks, changed defaults, maker updates and timely replacement reduce the risk."}
