      "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> (i) The owners work at the <b>strategic</b> level. (ii) The front office manager works at the <b>tactical</b> level. (iii) The housekeeping supervisor works at the <b>operational</b> level.</p><p><b>(b)</b> (i) The owners need summarised information, whereas the supervisor needs <b>detailed</b> information. (ii) For example, the owners use a five-year occupancy trend and tourism forecasts for St. Kitts. (iii) For example, the supervisor uses this morning's list of rooms to be cleaned.</p><p><b>(c)</b> (i) The towel order is a <b>structured</b> decision. (ii) I know this because it follows a fixed rule (order when stock falls below 200 towels), so the same thing happens every time and the laundry system can make the decision automatically.</p>",
      "teacherTip": "<p>This dial-down keeps the same resort and the same ideas as the full question, but splits each skill into one small step: name the level, make one contrast, classify one decision. Once a student scores well here, move them to the full version and ask for three contrasts in part (b) and all three decision types in part (c).</p>"
