     "up": {"title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Each piece of evidence below involves a trade-off or a less obvious principle. Choose the BEST evaluation in each case.</p>","items":[{"text":"After a redesign, experienced tellers at a Kingston bank need 3 keystrokes instead of 7 for a deposit, but new hires say the shortcut keys are not shown anywhere on screen. What does this show?","options":["Only ease of navigation changed","Both ease of use and ease of learning improved","Ease of use improved for experts, but ease of learning may have fallen for new hires","Alignment improved because there are fewer keystrokes"],"answer":2,"fb":"Fewer keystrokes help trained users; hidden shortcuts give new users nothing to recognise."},{"text":"A hospital in Bridgetown adds a confirmation step before a nurse saves a medication dose. Each entry now takes slightly longer. Which evaluation is BEST?","options":["It slightly slows entry but adds error prevention, which is part of ease of use and is justified where errors can harm patients","It only matters for ease of learning","It is an alignment improvement","It reduces ease of use, so it should be removed"],"answer":0,"fb":"Error prevention belongs to ease of use; in a hospital its benefit outweighs the extra second."},{"text":"A visitor to a government portal in Castries reaches the Pay Bill page but cannot tell which section she is in or how to return to the start. Which principle is weak?","options":["ease of use for experts","use of colour","ease of navigation","alignment"],"answer":2,"fb":"Knowing where you are and getting back are the core of navigation."},{"text":"A bank's voice assistant understands \"balance\" but not \"how much money do I have?\", so new callers must discover its exact words. Which principle is MOST reduced?","options":["ease of navigation","alignment","ease of learning","use of colour"],"answer":2,"fb":"Users must learn what the system understands, which lowers learnability; alignment and colour do not apply to speech."},{"text":"Which change would MOST improve ease of learning on a once-a-year tax form without slowing experienced accountants?","options":["Remove field labels to save space","Add example text inside fields and tooltips that experts can simply ignore","Replace the form with a command line","Force every user through a ten-step tutorial before each use"],"answer":1,"fb":"Built-in, optional help supports occasional users and costs experts nothing."},{"text":"A bill-payment kiosk lets first-time users succeed easily, but regular customers complain they cannot skip any screens. Which trade-off is this?","options":["High ease of use, but poor alignment","High ease of learning, but lower ease of use for frequent users","Poor use of colour for regular users","Poor navigation for everyone"],"answer":1,"fb":"A design that guides novices step by step can slow down people who already know it."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. Recognising trade-offs between ease of use and ease of learning, and knowing which principles do not apply to speech, is exactly what lifts an answer into the top band."}
