     "up": {"title":"Use or learning? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A port authority in Port of Spain replaces its command-line cargo system with a GUI. Clerks with ten years' experience say tasks now take twice as long, while new clerks are productive after one day instead of three weeks. Which conclusion is BEST supported by this evidence?","options":[{"t":"The GUI is better in every respect, because it is newer and easier to look at","fb":"The evidence shows a real loss of speed for experienced clerks, so \"better in every respect\" is not supported."},{"t":"The GUI reduced ease of learning, because experienced clerks are slower","fb":"This confuses the principles. Slower work by trained users is reduced ease of use; faster training for new clerks shows improved ease of learning."},{"t":"The GUI improved ease of learning but reduced ease of use for experts; adding keyboard shortcuts would help recover expert speed","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It identifies both effects accurately and proposes a fix that serves experts without losing the learnability gain."},{"t":"The change affected only alignment, because GUIs line items up on screen","fb":"Nothing in the evidence concerns layout. The measures given are training time and task time."}]}
