     "up": {"title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Adaptations across the region are being classified. Decide what kind of adaptive interface each one MAINLY is: does it replace the screen with sound or touch, detect a physical change as input, or adjust an existing interface through a built-in setting?</p>","main":"What kind of adaptive interface is this MAINLY?","bins":["Non-visual interface","Sensor","Accessibility feature"],"items":[{"t":"A motion sensor switches on the bathroom light for an older resident at night","bin":1,"fb":"It detects movement as input: sensor."},{"t":"A mono-audio setting lets a user who is deaf in one ear hear both stereo channels","bin":2,"fb":"A built-in operating-system setting: accessibility feature."},{"t":"A bank's telephone line reads a customer's balance aloud after a spoken request","bin":0,"fb":"Interaction entirely by sound: non-visual."},{"t":"A reduced-motion setting stops animations for a user who feels dizzy","bin":2,"fb":"A built-in setting adjusting the interface: accessibility feature."},{"t":"An on-screen keyboard switched on in the computer's settings for a user who cannot use a physical keyboard","bin":2,"fb":"A built-in setting: accessibility feature."},{"t":"A proximity sensor switches off a phone screen when it is held to the ear","bin":1,"fb":"It detects nearness as input: sensor."},{"t":"Talking announcements at bus stops: \"The next bus to Diamond arrives in 3 minutes\"","bin":0,"fb":"Information delivered by sound without a screen: non-visual."},{"t":"A fingerprint reader replaces typed passwords for a clerk with limited hand movement","bin":1,"fb":"A biometric sensor captures a physical feature as input."},{"t":"A smartwatch vibrates in patterns to tell a deaf-blind user which way to turn","bin":0,"fb":"Haptic output replaces the screen: non-visual."},{"t":"A phone's light sensor raises screen brightness automatically in the midday sun","bin":1,"fb":"It detects a physical change (light) as input: sensor."}],"win":"Well analysed. Non-visual interfaces replace the screen with sound or touch, sensors turn physical changes into input, and accessibility features are settings that adjust an existing interface."}
