     "up": {"title":"Non-visual or sensor? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"At Fort George Hotel, Feature X opens the room door automatically when it detects a guest approaching in a wheelchair. Feature Y is a small speaker that announces \"Your door is unlocked\" for blind guests. Which statement correctly classifies BOTH features?","options":[{"t":"X is a non-visual interface and Y is a sensor","fb":"This is reversed. X detects a physical change (approach) as input; Y gives output by sound instead of a screen."},{"t":"Both are accessibility features built into the operating system","fb":"Accessibility features are built-in settings in operating systems and apps; these are physical room devices with specific input and output roles."},{"t":"Both are sensors because both respond to the guest","fb":"Y does not detect anything; it outputs a spoken message, which makes it a non-visual interface."},{"t":"X is a sensor and Y is a non-visual interface","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. X uses a sensor to turn movement into input; Y uses audio output so the guest needs no screen."}]}
