     "up": {"title":"Hub or switch? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A school lab in Bridgetown has 24 PCs connected to a hub. During online tests the network slows badly, and a student running a packet-capture program can see other students' traffic. The technician considers four fixes. Which BEST solves BOTH problems?","options":[{"t":"Replace the hub with a switch, which forwards each frame only to the port of the destination MAC address","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Forwarding only to the intended port removes unnecessary traffic (speed) and stops other PCs receiving data meant for someone else (security)."},{"t":"Replace the hub with a modem","fb":"A modem converts signals for an Internet line; it cannot connect 24 PCs in a LAN."},{"t":"Replace the hub with a router, so that each PC gets an IP address","fb":"A router connects different networks. The fix inside one LAN is the switching function, which forwards by MAC address."},{"t":"Add a second hub and split the PCs between the two hubs","fb":"Each hub still broadcasts to all its ports, so traffic remains visible to every PC on it and collisions continue."}]}
