     "up": {"title":"Setting up the café network (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Boardwalk Bean Café in George Town wants a free customer hotspot, and its tills, stock system and office PC are on the same premises. Each decision builds on the last; one choice is only partly right.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How should the café's wireless networks be set up?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A separate, password-protected staff network for the tills and office, and an isolated guest hotspot.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Customers never share a network with business systems."},{"t":"One network, with its password printed on the menus so customers can join.","to":"bad1","fb":"A password everyone knows is effectively an open network shared with the tills."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The router connects the café to the Internet. What do you configure on it?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Switch on its firewall to block unrequested incoming connections, change the default admin password and install the latest firmware.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Filtering, a private admin password and patched firmware close the common gaps."},{"t":"Switch on the firewall but keep the default admin password, since only staff know where the router is.","to":"ok1","fb":"The firewall helps, but default admin passwords are published online and can be used over the network."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The manager wants to check stock figures from home at night. How should she connect?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Through a remote-desktop service opened directly to the Internet with a short shared password.","to":"bad2","fb":"This exposes the stock system to password-guessing attacks from anywhere."},{"t":"Through a VPN with her own log-in, which encrypts the connection to the café network.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Secure, authenticated remote access over the public Internet."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>At lunchtime, 40 customers stream video on the hotspot and the tills slow down. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Limit the bandwidth each guest device may use and give the staff network priority, then check whether the Internet plan needs upgrading.","to":"good","fb":"Right. Bandwidth is shared; managing it protects the business while keeping the hotspot useful."},{"t":"Let customers join the staff network when the hotspot is busy.","to":"bad3","fb":"That undoes the separation and exposes the tills."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Secure and practical.</b> The hotspot serves customers, the tills stay protected behind a firewall and keep their bandwidth, and the manager works remotely through a VPN. You justified each measure by what it protects, which is what Paper 02 rewards.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Half-secured.</b> A customer on the guest network finds the router's admin page and logs in with the factory password. Nothing is stolen this time, but settings are changed. Try again and replace the default password.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Exposed tills.</b> A customer's infected laptop reaches the till system. Try again and separate staff and guest networks.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Broken in.</b> Automated password guessing finds the short password within hours. Try again and use a VPN.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Separation lost.</b> Guests on the staff network can now reach the tills. Try again and manage bandwidth instead.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
