     "up": {"title":"Paper 02 practice: networking a Caribbean business (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Paper 02 structured question: network types, devices, security and communication (20 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Island Breeze Hotels group has a hotel in Rodney Bay, St. Lucia, and another in Anguilla. Each hotel has about 40 staff computers, card-key systems and a guest Wi-Fi hotspot. Head office in Castries wants staff in both hotels to share booking records, managers to work from home during the hurricane season, and the group's food suppliers to log in to see orders. Weekly management meetings currently require six managers to fly between the islands, costing about US$1,800 a week. The Anguilla hotel's Internet link is 20 Mbps and is shared with guests.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, using the group to illustrate each. <i>(3 marks)</i></p><p>(b) <b>Explain</b> the function of a switch and a router in the group's network. <i>(4 marks)</i></p><p>(c) <b>Explain</b> how the group could use an intranet and an extranet, and identify ONE security risk the extranet creates. <i>(3 marks)</i></p><p>(d) <b>Explain</b> how a firewall and a VPN would protect the network when managers telecommute. <i>(4 marks)</i></p><p>(e) <b>Evaluate</b> replacing the weekly travel with videoconferencing, referring to duplex transmission, bandwidth and the Anguilla link. <i>(6 marks)</i></p>","min":400,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A LAN ..., for example ... A WAN ..., for example ...<br><b>(b)</b> A switch ... A router ...<br><b>(c)</b> The intranet ... The extranet ... One risk is ...<br><b>(d)</b> A firewall ... A VPN ...<br><b>(e)</b> Videoconferencing would save ... It needs duplex because ... and high bandwidth because ... However, the Anguilla link ... This could be solved by ... Overall, ...</p>","checklist":["I compared LAN and WAN on area and gave a hotel example of each.","I explained the switch (MAC, within a LAN) and the router (IP, between networks).","I matched intranet to staff and extranet to suppliers, and named a real extranet risk.","I explained what a firewall filters and how a VPN encrypts.","I weighed savings against the Anguilla bandwidth problem and suggested a solution.","I ended part (e) with a justified judgement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"(a) LAN versus WAN","marks":3,"look":["LAN","WAN","building|site|one hotel|local","countr|island|Anguilla|wide|leased"],"min":4,"strong":"LAN and WAN distinguished with hotel examples.","weak":"A LAN covers one site (the Rodney Bay hotel); a WAN links sites across a wide area (both hotels and head office) using leased links."},{"criterion":"(b) Switch and router","marks":4,"look":["switch","MAC|only to|intended|specific","router","IP|between networks|different networks|Internet|path|route"],"min":4,"strong":"Both devices explained by function and addressing.","weak":"A switch links devices in one hotel LAN and sends data only to the destination MAC address; a router links the hotel LAN to the WAN and Internet and forwards packets by IP address."},{"criterion":"(c) Intranet, extranet and risk","marks":3,"look":["intranet.{0,120}(staff|employee|internal)|(staff|employee|internal).{0,120}intranet","extranet.{0,120}(supplier|outside|partner)|(supplier|outside|partner).{0,120}extranet","risk|stolen|password|account|access|breach|former supplier"],"min":3,"strong":"Correct users for each and a realistic risk.","weak":"Intranet: staff share bookings and rosters. Extranet: suppliers see orders. Risk: a supplier's stolen password or an account left active after a contract ends could give outsiders a way in."},{"criterion":"(d) Firewall and VPN","marks":4,"look":["firewall","filter|rule|block|unauthori","VPN","encrypt|tunnel|authenticat"],"min":4,"strong":"Both measures explained for remote working.","weak":"A firewall filters traffic by rules between the hotel network and the Internet; a VPN encrypts the manager's connection and authenticates her."},{"criterion":"(e) Evaluation of videoconferencing","marks":6,"look":["1,?800|travel|cost|ferry|fly|time","duplex|same time|simultaneous","bandwidth|capacity|mbps","20 ?mbps|anguilla|shared|guest","upgrade|prioriti|separate|limit|dedicated|audio","overall|on balance|recommend|judge"],"min":5,"strong":"Savings weighed against technical limits, with a solution and a verdict.","weak":"Weigh the savings (about US$1,800 and travel time each week) against the needs (full duplex, high bandwidth), explain that the Anguilla 20 Mbps link shared with guests may freeze the video, propose a fix (prioritise staff traffic, limit guest bandwidth or upgrade) and reach a judgement."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A <b>LAN</b> covers a small area such as one building or site and is usually owned by one organisation, for example the network linking the 40 staff computers inside the Rodney Bay hotel. A <b>WAN</b> covers a wide geographical area, often across countries, using links leased from telecom providers, for example the network linking the Rodney Bay hotel, the Anguilla hotel and head office in Castries.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A <b>switch</b> is the central device in each hotel LAN. It learns the MAC address of the device on each port and sends data only to the intended computer, reducing unnecessary traffic and improving security. A <b>router</b> connects different networks: it links each hotel LAN to the group WAN and the Internet and forwards packets between them using IP addresses, choosing a path so that booking records reach the other hotel.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The <b>intranet</b> is a private, staff-only network using web technologies, where employees in both hotels share booking records, rosters and policies. The <b>extranet</b> opens part of it to authorised outsiders, so food suppliers can log in to see orders. One risk is that a supplier's password could be stolen, or a supplier's account left active after its contract ends, giving outsiders a route into the group's systems; suppliers must see only their own section.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A <b>firewall</b> sits between the hotel network and the Internet and filters traffic against rules, blocking unrequested and unauthorised connections while allowing legitimate traffic. A <b>VPN</b> creates an encrypted tunnel over the Internet from the manager's home to the office network, so intercepted data cannot be read, and it authenticates the manager before access is granted.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Videoconferencing would save about US$1,800 a week in fares, plus the managers' travel time, and meetings could continue when storms stop flights. It needs <b>full duplex</b> transmission so that managers can speak and hear each other at the same time, as in a real meeting, and high <b>bandwidth</b> because live video sends a large amount of data every second. The weakness is the Anguilla link: 20 Mbps shared with guests could easily be used up by guests streaming in the evening, so the video would freeze and the meeting would fail. This can be solved by giving staff traffic priority, limiting guest bandwidth during meetings or upgrading the link, with an audio teleconference as a fallback. Overall, I judge videoconferencing worthwhile, because the savings are large and regular, provided the Anguilla bandwidth problem is fixed first.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>The Challenge version turns part (e) into an evaluation that must use the stimulus figures. Award the top marks only when students connect the 20 Mbps shared link to the risk of frozen video and propose a realistic fix. In (b), still insist on \"between networks\" for the router.</p>"}
