     "up": {"title":"Standards and layers in action (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Mr. Hodge now gives his Form 6 class harder questions that apply the ideas of standards, layers and encapsulation to real problems.</p>","items":[{"text":"A firm in Port of Spain replaces its copper LAN with fibre. Which layers must change?","options":["Only the lowest layers; applications such as email are unaffected","Only the Application layer","The Transport and Session layers","All seven layers"],"answer":0,"fb":"Layer independence: changing the medium affects the Physical (Network Access) level, not the protocols above."},{"text":"Why can a phone made in Korea browse a website hosted in Barbados through a router made in China?","options":["All three makers use the same factory","Each device follows the same published standards at every layer, so they interoperate","Routers translate between different makers' private protocols","The website converts itself for each phone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Agreed standards at every layer are what make interoperability possible."},{"text":"What is the difference between a protocol and a standard?","options":["They mean exactly the same thing","Standards apply only to cables","Protocols are hardware; standards are software","A standard is a protocol or specification that has been formally agreed and published; not every protocol is a formal standard"],"answer":3,"fb":"Formal agreement and publication make a protocol a standard that any maker can build to."},{"text":"At the receiver, each layer removes its own header as the data moves up. This is called...","options":["compression","routing","encapsulation","de-encapsulation"],"answer":3,"fb":"Headers are added going down at the sender (encapsulation) and removed going up at the receiver (de-encapsulation)."},{"text":"A technician finds the cable is fine and the laptop has a valid IP address. Pinging the server's IP address works, but web pages will not load by name. Which layer should she check next?","options":["Network","Application","Data Link","Physical"],"answer":1,"fb":"Lower layers are proven working; the fault is likely in an Application-layer service such as DNS or the browser."},{"text":"Why is the OSI model still taught when the Internet runs on TCP/IP?","options":["TCP/IP has no Transport layer","It is faster than TCP/IP","Most routers use only OSI protocols","It is a detailed reference model for designing, teaching and troubleshooting"],"answer":3,"fb":"Its seven separate layers make it a precise reference, even though few systems implement each layer separately."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied layering to change, interoperability and troubleshooting, and you separated protocols from standards and encapsulation from de-encapsulation."}
