     "up": {"title":"Design the network for Bay View Guesthouse (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Bay View Guesthouse near Fort George has grown. Mr. Arrindell now needs the reception PC, the office PC and a card machine on the network, a till at the new pool bar 140 m from the office, and Wi-Fi for staff and guests. Some choices are only partly right.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>He still has an old 8-port hub. What should be at the centre of the LAN?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A switch, because it forwards frames only to the destination port, which is faster and keeps traffic private.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. A switch is the correct centre for a star LAN."},{"t":"The hub, to save money.","to":"bad1","fb":"A hub repeats every frame to every port, wasting bandwidth and exposing card data."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The pool-bar till is 140 m from the office switch. How do you connect it?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One 140 m Cat6 cable straight to the till.","to":"bad2","fb":"That exceeds the 100 m limit for copper Ethernet, so the link will be unreliable."},{"t":"A fibre-optic link to a small switch at the pool bar, or a second switch part-way so that no copper run exceeds 100 m.","to":"n3","fb":"Right. Copper Ethernet runs must stay within 100 m."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>How do you set up the Wi-Fi?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One open network with the SSID hidden.","to":"bad3","fb":"Hiding the SSID is not security, and an open network sends everything unencrypted."},{"t":"Two SSIDs without personal details: staff with WPA3 (AES) and a strong key, guests with a separate key and isolation from the staff LAN; the card machine stays wired on the staff side.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Separation, encryption and a wired card machine protect the business."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>On the router you find the password \"admin\", no firewall rules and remote administration switched on. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Change the router password only; the rest can wait.","to":"ok1","fb":"A good first step, but without firewall rules and with remote administration on, the network is still exposed from the Internet."},{"t":"Set a strong router password, enable firewall rules that block unrequested inbound traffic, turn off remote administration and update the firmware.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Every common route into the router is closed."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Secure and working.</b> A switch at the centre, distances respected, separate encrypted wireless networks and a hardened router: a top-band design answer justifies each of these choices against the guesthouse's needs.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Partly secured.</b> Attackers on the Internet keep probing the router's remote-administration page and unfiltered ports. Nothing is lost yet, but the risk remains. Try again and complete the router hardening.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow and exposed.</b> Collisions slow the card machine, and any device can capture card traffic. Try again with a switch.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Dropped sales.</b> The over-length cable gives intermittent errors and the pool-bar till keeps going offline. Try again and respect the 100 m limit.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Breach.</b> A visitor in the car park joins the open network and reads unencrypted traffic. Try again with separate, encrypted SSIDs.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
