     "type": "mcq",
     "title": "Reading a design",
     "q": "In a network diagram for Dominica Coffee Exporters, the designer draws a brick-wall shape between the router and the switch. What does this shape represent, and why is it placed there?",
     "options": [
      {
       "t": "A firewall, placed there so all traffic between the WAN and the LAN is filtered",
       "ok": true,
       "fb": "Correct. The brick wall is the standard firewall symbol. Placing it at the WAN–LAN boundary means every packet entering or leaving the LAN is checked against its rules."
      },
      {
       "t": "A wireless access point, placed there to broadcast the SSID",
       "fb": "An access point is drawn with antennas, and it connects to the switch on the LAN side."
      },
      {
       "t": "An Ethernet patch panel, placed there to organise cables",
       "fb": "A patch panel is not one of the standard shapes in the syllabus, and the brick wall is a security symbol."
      },
      {
       "t": "A hub, placed there to repeat signals to every PC",
       "fb": "A hub is drawn as a box with arrows, and it would not be placed between the router and the switch for protection."
      }
     ],
     "tutor": [
      {
       "h": "Think of a real wall",
       "p": "<p>A wall keeps things out unless they pass through a gate. Which device does that with network traffic?</p>"
      },
      {
       "h": "Position matters",
       "p": "<p>Between the router (outside) and the switch (inside) is the WAN–LAN boundary.</p>"
      }
     ],
