     "up": {"title":"Hub or router? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"At St. John's Harbour Tours, a technician connected the ISP's fibre terminal to one of the router's LAN ports and connected the hub to the router's WAN port. Now no PC can reach the Internet, and ipconfig on each PC shows a 169.254 address. What is the BEST fix?","options":[{"t":"Swap the connections: fibre terminal to the WAN port and the hub to a LAN port","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. With the hub on the WAN port, the PCs cannot reach the router's DHCP server, so they give themselves 169.254 addresses; with the ISP line on a LAN port, the router has no Internet connection. Swapping fixes both."},{"t":"Fit terminators to the hub","fb":"Terminators belong on a bus backbone, not a hub in a star."},{"t":"Give every PC a fixed address in the 169.254 range","fb":"169.254 addresses are a symptom of failed DHCP; they cannot reach a gateway or the Internet."},{"t":"Replace the hub with a switch","fb":"A switch would improve privacy and speed, but the cables would still be in the wrong ports."}]}
