     "up": {"title":"Match the security setting to its job (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each description says what a setting PREVENTS or ACHIEVES. Match each security setting to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Router password","r":"Stops a guest who knows the Wi-Fi key from switching off the encryption"},{"l":"Network key","r":"Changed when a receptionist leaves, so her phone can no longer join"},{"l":"AES","r":"The cipher that makes captured wireless frames unreadable"},{"l":"WPA3","r":"Its SAE handshake forces every password guess to be a live attempt"},{"l":"MAC filtering","r":"Refuses a laptop whose hardware address is not listed, though addresses can be spoofed"},{"l":"Hardware firewall","r":"Blocks unrequested inbound connections for every device on the LAN"},{"l":"Software firewall","r":"Protects one laptop even from other devices on the same LAN"},{"l":"Guest SSID","r":"Gives visitors Internet access with no route to the office PCs"},{"l":"Firmware update","r":"Closes known weaknesses in the router's own software"}],"win":"Very good. Each setting protects a different target: the router, entry to the Wi-Fi, data in the air, the boundary, individual hosts or the separation of visitors."}
