     "up": {"title":"Access control all around us (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These questions apply the definitions of access control to less obvious situations at home and in business.</p>","items":[{"text":"A hotel's nightly billing program is allowed to read bookings but not delete them. Which idea is being applied?","options":["Only people can be users","Users may be processes, and each user should get only the rights it needs","Programs do not need access control","Encryption replaces access control"],"answer":1,"fb":"Processes are users too, and least privilege limits what each can do."},{"text":"A certificate installed on a company laptop vouches for that computer when it connects to the bank's network. This is...","options":["a biometric","a PIN","a digital certificate","a hardware token"],"answer":2,"fb":"Digital certificates are electronic credentials that vouch for a user or computer."},{"text":"Which pair correctly classifies the resources?","options":["Both are computing resources","Server room: computing resource; customer database: physical asset","Both are physical assets","Server room: physical asset; customer database: computing resource"],"answer":3,"fb":"Rooms and buildings are physical assets; systems and data are computing resources."},{"text":"A cashier is promoted to supervisor, and refund rights are added to her account. Which phase is this?","options":["Policy definition","Policy enforcement","Identification","Accountability"],"answer":0,"fb":"Changing what an identity may do is policy definition (authorisation)."},{"text":"The next day, the till accepts her refund approval. Which phase is this?","options":["Policy definition","Enrolment","Policy enforcement","Encryption"],"answer":2,"fb":"Granting a request using rights already defined is enforcement."},{"text":"A family in San Nicolas gives a spare house key to a neighbour. In access-control terms, this...","options":["extends authorised use to another person, but the lock cannot record who used the key","turns the lock into a biometric","removes all access control from the house","is an example of encryption"],"answer":0,"fb":"The neighbour is authorised, but a simple lock provides no accountability."},{"text":"Why is the IT definition of access control broader than \"locking doors\"?","options":["It only covers buildings","It regulates who or what can view or use resources in a computing environment, including processes and data","It applies only to passwords","It only covers mobile phones"],"answer":1,"fb":"It covers people and processes, systems and data, not just physical entry."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied least privilege to processes, classified resources and phases correctly, and recognised what simple controls such as keys cannot provide."}
