     "up": {"title":"Key cards: what do they really prove? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"An unauthorised visitor entered the server room at Port of Spain Data Centre using a proximity card an employee had left in a café. Which change would MOST directly prevent this from happening again?","options":[{"t":"Replace the proximity cards with swipe cards","fb":"Swipe cards still prove only possession; a found swipe card would work just as well."},{"t":"Review the entry log at the end of each month","fb":"Reviewing logs helps detect misuse, but only after the visitor is already inside."},{"t":"Print the cards in a brighter colour so they are less likely to be lost","fb":"This might reduce losses slightly, but it does not stop a found card being used."},{"t":"Require a PIN or fingerprint at the reader as well as the card","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Adding a second factor means a found card alone no longer opens the door."}]}
