     "up": {"title":"Password clinic (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>The Flamboyant Heights College students now want the numbers behind password advice. Answer each question.</p>","items":[{"text":"How many combinations does an 8-character password made only of the digits 0 to 9 have?","options":["100 million (10<sup>8</sup>)","62<sup>8</sup>","8<sup>10</sup>","80"],"answer":0,"fb":"Ten choices at each of 8 positions: 10<sup>8</sup> = 100,000,000."},{"text":"Moving from 26 lower-case letters to 62 mixed-case alphanumeric characters at each of 8 positions multiplies the combinations by about...","options":["a thousand","36","2","a million"],"answer":0,"fb":"(62 ÷ 26)<sup>8</sup> is about 1,000, as the notes show: 209 billion becomes about 218 trillion."},{"text":"Which change adds the MOST strength to \"Coral7Reef\"?","options":["Add six more unrelated characters","Replace one e with 3","Change the 7 to an 8","Make the C lower case"],"answer":0,"fb":"Each extra position multiplies the combinations, so length adds the most."},{"text":"Why is \"Three#Pelicans9Bay\" both strong and memorable?","options":["It is long, mixes character types, has a special character early, and comes from a phrase the user can picture","It is short","It uses the user's birth year","It is all capitals"],"answer":0,"fb":"Built from a passphrase, it is long and varied but easy to recall."},{"text":"What is the difference between masking and encryption?","options":["Masking applies only to PINs","Masking scrambles the stored password; encryption hides it on screen","They are the same","Masking hides characters on screen; encryption scrambles the stored or transmitted password"],"answer":3,"fb":"Masking stops onlookers; encryption protects the data itself."},{"text":"A 4-digit PIN has only 10,000 combinations. Why is it still acceptable for a bank card?","options":["PINs are encrypted on the card's stripe","PINs are never guessed","The card must also be present, and it is locked or retained after three wrong attempts","10,000 is a very large number"],"answer":2,"fb":"Two factors plus lockout make guessing impractical."}],"win":"Excellent. You can calculate combinations, explain why length matters most, and show how lockout and a second factor make even short PINs workable."}
