     "up": {"title":"Securing the clinic (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are the IT consultant for the Harbourview Medical Clinic on Bridge Street. This time there are more decisions, including remote access and staff leaving, and some answers are only partly right.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The server sits under the reception desk where patients wait. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Leave it there; a strong password is enough.","to":"bad1","fb":"A password does nothing if the server is carried away."},{"t":"Move it into the doctor's office, which has an ordinary lock and a key shared by all staff.","to":"ok1","fb":"Better than reception, but a shared metal key cannot be cancelled or show who entered."},{"t":"Move it to a locked room opened only by staff key cards, with a UPS.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Physical control and power protection come first."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Everyone logs in with the shared account \"clinic\" and password \"harbour1\". What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep the shared account but change the password to \"Harbour2\".","to":"bad2","fb":"Still obvious, and still no accountability."},{"t":"Unique user IDs, effective passwords, role-based access rights and an audit trail.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. This gives authentication, least privilege and accountability."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Doctors want to check results from home in the evening. How should they log in?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Two-factor authentication (password plus a code on their phone) over an encrypted connection.","to":"n4","fb":"Right. Sensitive data reached over the Internet needs two factors and encryption."},{"t":"Their normal password over the Internet.","to":"bad3","fb":"A single factor over the Internet is too weak for patient records."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Backups go to a USB drive in the server room, and patient forms often contain impossible birth dates. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Off-site backups, but unencrypted to keep things simple.","to":"ok2","fb":"Disaster recovery improves, but a lost backup drive would expose every patient's record, and the form problem remains."},{"t":"Encrypted backups stored off-site, with the key kept separately and securely, and validation checks on the patient form.","to":"n5","fb":"Strong. Confidentiality, disaster recovery and integrity are all addressed."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>A receptionist resigns on Friday. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Disable her accounts at the end of the month with the payroll run.","to":"bad4","fb":"For weeks, a former employee could still enter and log in."},{"t":"Cancel her key card and disable her log-in on her last day.","to":"good","fb":"Right. Facilities and IT close both kinds of access together."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Layered and complete.</b> Physical controls, unique authenticated log-ins, least privilege, two-factor remote access, encrypted off-site backups, validation and a clean exit process. A top answer justifies each layer against a specific risk.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Partly secure.</b> The server is out of public reach, but when a copy of the key goes missing nobody knows who has it or who entered. Try again with key cards.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Exposed copy.</b> The off-site drive is left in a car and stolen; every record on it is readable. Try again and encrypt the backups.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Server stolen.</b> A visitor walks out with it. Try again and start with physical control.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Untraceable change.</b> An allergy record is altered and the log shows only \"clinic\". Try again with unique IDs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Account taken over.</b> A doctor's reused password leaks from another site and an attacker reads results from abroad. Try again with two factors.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Former employee inside.</b> She logs in from home and copies patient lists. Try again and close access on her last day.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
