     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Data security is the protection of data from unauthorised access, change, disclosure or loss, whether deliberate or accidental. For example, the credit union keeps its server in a locked room and gives each teller a unique log-in, so members' account balances cannot be viewed by outsiders.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Authentication is the process of verifying that a user is who they claim to be before access is granted. It uses something the user knows (a password), has (a key card or phone) or is (a fingerprint). For example, a teller enters her password and then a one-time code sent to her phone, which is two-factor authentication.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Encryption is converting readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext using an algorithm and a key, so that only someone with the correct key can decrypt it. For example, members' details sent from the St. George's branch to the head office are encrypted, so anyone intercepting them sees only scrambled characters.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Data corruption is the unintended alteration of data during saving, storage or transmission, making it incorrect or unreadable. It can be caused by a power cut while a file is being saved or by a failing hard drive. For example, if the electricity fails while the day's deposits are being saved, the deposits file may not open the next morning.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Data integrity is the accuracy, completeness and consistency of data throughout its life. It is maintained by validation, verification and backups. For example, a range check rejects a loan amount above the member's approved limit, and a supervisor verifies large deposits against the paper slip.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>\"Explain\" questions usually carry two marks per term: one for an accurate definition and one for development (how, why or an example). A one-line definition caps you at half marks. Use the scenario in every part.</p>",
