     "up": {"title":"From defence network to World Wide Web (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The Lower Sixth timeline now includes later developments. Put all eight events in the order they happened.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The US Department of Defense funds ARPANET (1969) to link research computers."},{"t":"Networks around the world are joined using the TCP/IP protocols, forming the Internet."},{"t":"At CERN in 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee proposes a hypertext system so researchers can locate and share data."},{"t":"The first web server and browser link hypertext documents across the Internet."},{"t":"Early web pages carry mainly text joined by hyperlinks."},{"t":"Browsers call plug-ins to add animation, sound and video to pages."},{"t":"HTML5 lets browsers play video and audio themselves, and Flash support ends in 2020."},{"t":"Hyperlinks now reach video, interactive programs, conferencing and online gaming in the browser."}],"win":"Exactly right. A defence network became the Internet, the web was built on top of it, and its multimedia moved from plug-ins to built-in HTML5 support."}
