     "up": {"title":"Spot the correct HTML statements (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jaden has written a harder set of notes on HTML structure. Read each statement carefully; some contain one wrong detail.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY statement that is correct.","lines":["1. &lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hot&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; is correctly nested.","2. &lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hot&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/b&gt; is correctly nested.","3. In &lt;a href=\"prices.html\"&gt;Prices&lt;/a&gt;, href is an attribute and Prices is the content.","4. The text of the title element appears as a heading at the top of the page body.","5. An element is made of a start tag, its content and an end tag.","6. Attribute values should be written inside quotation marks.","7. The img element needs a closing &lt;/img&gt; tag.","8. One element can have several attributes, separated by spaces.","9. The head section is where visible paragraphs belong."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,7],"win":"Correct. Nesting closes the last element opened first; href is an attribute in the start tag; an element is start tag, content and end tag; values go in quotation marks; several attributes may be used. The title appears on the browser tab, img is empty, and visible content belongs in the body.","miss":"Not quite. Check the nesting order, where the title appears, whether img has an end tag and which section holds visible content."}
