     "up": {"title":"Lists and images quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Shanice's hurricane-preparedness page now needs more precise list and image code.</p>","items":[{"text":"Which start tag creates a numbered list that begins at 5?","options":["&lt;ol type=\"5\"&gt;","&lt;ol start=\"5\"&gt;","&lt;ol type=\"i\"&gt;","&lt;ul start=\"5\"&gt;"],"answer":1,"fb":"start sets the first number; type sets the style of numbering."},{"text":"She wants a sub-list under \"Torch and batteries\". Where does the inner ul go?","options":["After the closing outer ul","Before the outer ul","Inside the li for \"Torch and batteries\"","Inside the head"],"answer":2,"fb":"Nested lists are placed inside the list item they belong to."},{"text":"Why set width and height on an img?","options":["They are required for the image to show","They make the file smaller","They replace the alt text","They reserve space, so the page does not jump while the image loads"],"answer":3,"fb":"Reserved space keeps the layout stable."},{"text":"What does the browser do when it reads an img element's src?","options":["It converts the text into a picture","It opens a plug-in","It finds the picture stored inside the HTML file","It makes a separate HTTP request for the image file"],"answer":3,"fb":"Images are separate files fetched with their own requests."},{"text":"Which type value numbers a list i, ii, iii?","options":["type=\"i\"","type=\"I\"","type=\"a\"","type=\"1\""],"answer":0,"fb":"Lower-case i gives lower-case Roman numerals."},{"text":"What does the title attribute on an img do?","options":["Replaces alt for screen readers","Names the image file","Shows tooltip text when the mouse hovers over the image","Sets the page title on the browser tab"],"answer":2,"fb":"title gives a tooltip; alt is the alternative text."},{"text":"In a description list of storm terms, how is each entry built?","options":["dt for each term and dd for its description","li for each term","th for each term","td for each term"],"answer":0,"fb":"dt holds the term and dd holds its description."}],"win":"Excellent. You controlled numbering, nested lists correctly and understood how images are requested, sized and described."}
