  "title": "Paper 02 practice: networking a Portsmouth clinic",
  "genre": "Paper 02 structured response: IS components, network types, simple network design, security mechanisms",
  "stimulus": "<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p><p class=\"passage\">The <b>Portsmouth Family Clinic</b> has a reception desk, two consulting rooms and a small pharmacy. Staff currently keep patient cards in a filing cabinet and use one stand-alone PC for billing. The clinic plans to install four PCs, a shared laser printer and Wi-Fi for staff tablets, connected to the Internet through a Flow business line. Doctors want to share a patient database, send prescriptions to the pharmacy, and read test results from a laboratory in Roseau through a secure web portal. The manager is worried about patient confidentiality because the old PC uses the password \"clinic1\".</p>",
  "prompt": "<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe how <b>THREE</b> components of an information system (from hardware, software, data, procedures, users and network) would work together when a doctor records a prescription. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between: <ol type=\"i\"><li>a LAN and a WAN <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>an intranet and an extranet, using the laboratory portal as an example <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Design a simple network for the clinic. Describe your diagram, naming the devices and connections, and state <b>TWO</b> wireless security settings you would configure. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Compare a biometric fingerprint reader with a password as ways of controlling access to the patient database, and explain the term <b>data integrity</b>. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
  "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> The doctor (user) follows the procedure ... using the PC (hardware) running ... (software) ... the data ... travels over the network to ...<br><b>(b)(i)</b> A LAN ... whereas a WAN ... <b>(ii)</b> An intranet ... whereas an extranet ... e.g. the laboratory portal ...<br><b>(c)</b> [Diagram] The Flow line connects to a router with a firewall ... a switch ... PC 1–4 ... printer ... wireless access point broadcasting SSID ... Security: WPA3/WPA2 with AES ... MAC filtering ... change the router password ...<br><b>(d)</b> A fingerprint reader ... whereas a password ... Data integrity means ...</p>",
  "checklist": [
   "I showed three IS components interacting, not just listed them.",
   "I gave a clear contrast for LAN/WAN and intranet/extranet with a clinic example.",
   "My network design names a router, firewall, switch, wireless access point, PCs and printer and how they connect.",
   "I named two specific wireless security settings (e.g. WPA3 with AES, MAC filtering, strong network key).",
   "I compared biometrics and passwords and defined data integrity."
  ],
