   "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Hardware:</b> the doctor types the prescription on the consulting-room PC. <b>Software:</b> the shared patient database application stores and sends the prescription. <b>Users:</b> the doctor enters the prescription and the pharmacist reads it to give out the medicine.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A <b>LAN</b> covers a small area such as one building; an example is the network linking the reception, consulting rooms and pharmacy inside the clinic. A <b>WAN</b> covers a large geographical area; an example is the connection from the clinic over the Internet to the laboratory in Roseau.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A <b>router</b> connects the clinic's network to the Internet through the Flow line. A <b>switch</b> connects the four PCs and the laser printer with Ethernet cables so they can share data. A <b>wireless access point</b> provides Wi-Fi so staff tablets can join the network.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A fingerprint is unique to each person and cannot be guessed or shared, whereas \"clinic1\" is weak and easy to guess.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Data integrity means that data is accurate, complete and consistent, and has not been changed without authorisation.</p>",
   "stimulus": "<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p><p class=\"passage\">The <b>Portsmouth Family Clinic</b> has a reception desk, two consulting rooms and a small pharmacy. Staff currently keep patient cards in a filing cabinet and use one stand-alone PC for billing. The clinic plans to install four PCs, a shared laser printer and Wi-Fi for staff tablets, connected to the Internet through a Flow business line. Doctors want to share a patient database, send prescriptions to the pharmacy, and read test results from a laboratory in Roseau through a secure web portal. The manager is worried about patient confidentiality because the old PC uses the password \"clinic1\".</p>",
